 back to `~` *before* converting `~1` back to `/`. The JSON Pointer spec (RFC 6901, section 4) requires the reverse order. With the wrong order, the escaped text `~01` should become the literal `~1` but becomes `/` instead:\n\n```\nunescape(\"~01\")   -> \"/\"      (should be \"~1\")\n```\n\nWhat that breaks:\n- `JsonPointer(\"/~01\")` points to the key `\"/\"` instead of `\"~1\"`, so it reads or writes the wrong key in the document.\n- Converting a pointer to a string and back changes it, e.g. `JsonPointer.from_parts([\"~1\"]).parts == [\"/\"]`.\n- `join` gets it wrong the same way.\n\nThe 9 failing tests are the `~01` cases in unescaping, round-tripping, parsing, resolving, setting, `from_parts` and `join`. The fix would be one line, which I didn't make because you asked me not to change the library:\n\n```python\ndef unescape(s):\n    return s.replace('~1', '/').replace('~0', '~')\n```\n\nThe passing tests cover:\n- **Escaping:** `escape`/`unescape` and round-trips.\n- **Parsing:** pointers that don't start with `/`, invalid escapes (`~`, `~2`, `~a`), empty segments.\n- **Reading values:** every example document from the spec, defaults (including `None` and other falsy values), missing keys, the detailed versus short error messages, list index rules (leading zeros, signs, spaces, out of range), `-` (end of list), strings and numbers that can't be indexed, and custom objects that support `[]`.\n- **Setting values:** in place and on a copy, appending with `-`, setting the root, tuples and strings that can't be assigned to.\n- **Other operations:** `to_last`, `get_part`, `path`/`str`/`repr`, equality and hashing, `from_parts`, `join` and `/`, `contains` and `in`, `pairwise`, and the `EndOfList` text form.\n\nIf you need the run to come out green before the library is fixed, I can mark those 9 tests as expected failures (`xfail(strict=True)`). They would then fail again as soon as the bug is fixed, so nobody forgets to remove the marker.","ttft_ms":3146,"type":"resul